# Generalization

**Generalization** is the widening of a concept or a claim so that it covers
more cases than it did. Vector spaces generalize to modules when the scalars
are allowed to form a ring. The operation acts on the reach of an idea. It may
be carried out with no observations at all, which separates it from
[[induction]], and it keeps the original cases as special cases, which
separates it from [[analogy]].

## Description

### Two lanes

The discussion meets generalization in two places that should be told apart.

| | Generalizing a construction | Generalizing as a task |
|---|---|---|
| Where | mathematics | work with language models |
| Example | addition to multiplication to exponentiation; vector spaces to modules | from specific cases to a broader applicable principle |
| Status of result | a definition or theorem | a proposal to be checked |
| Old cases | recovered exactly as special cases | covered if the proposal is right |

**In mathematics.** The question that opened this thread listed the
"connections" mathematics is full of: the first, second and third derivative;
the passage from sum to product to exponent; complements and dual spaces. The
second of these is generalization in the plainest sense. Multiplication is
iterated addition, exponentiation iterated multiplication, tetration iterated
exponentiation, and the [[hyperoperation]] hierarchy is the general term. The
reply calls this *operator lifting*: an operation becomes the input of a
higher-order construction. The other standard form is weakening an
assumption, as when fields give way to rings and vector spaces to modules.

**As a task.** In the table of operations a language model is asked to
perform, generalization takes specific cases or concepts and returns a broader
applicable principle. Here nothing guarantees the result. It is a candidate,
and it joins the hypotheses that [[evaluation]] has to sort.

### Generation, the little brother

The same question called generation "a little brother of generalization". The
two run in opposite directions along one axis.

| Operation | Input | Output |
|---|---|---|
| Generalization | specific cases or concepts | broader principle |
| Specialization | general concept and constraints | restricted formulation |
| Generation | prompt, model or rule | new instances |

Generalization goes from instances to a rule. [[generation]] goes from a rule
to instances, and so does [[specialization]], which narrows a concept by
adding constraints. Generation is the little brother in the sense that it
needs the rule to exist already: it spends what generalization earned. A
language model generates fluently, and that fluency is easy to mistake for the
harder operation that produced the rule.

### What generalization is not

- **Not induction.** Induction is an inferential method based on evidence.
  Generalization broadens a concept or claim and may be purely mathematical.
  An inductive conclusion often has the form of a generalization, but the
  inference and the form are different things.
- **Not abstraction.** Abstraction identifies a structure apart from detail.
  Generalization extends a structure already identified. The discussion's
  first table put them on one line, and its later tables give each its own.
- **Not summarization.** The question mentioned the two together as tasks for
  a language model. A summary covers the same cases in fewer words. A
  generalization covers more cases.

### Generalizing a definition

The discussion ends by doing to the [[strange-loop]] what this card describes.
Starting from Hofstadter's sense, it proposes six wider or sharper variants
and asks, for each, what was included or left out. That list is in
[[definitions-of-strange-loop]]. It shows the risk of the operation as well: a
definition generalized far enough to cover every dynamic system no longer
picks out anything, which was the correction made earlier in the same
discussion.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: specific cases in, broader principle out.
- It is not [[induction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a widening of scope, against an inference from evidence.
- It is distinct from [[abstraction]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it extends a structure that abstraction found.
- It is not [[summarization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: more cases, against fewer words.
- Its inverse is [[specialization]] {relation: contrasts}, and its little brother is [[generation]] {relation: contrasts}.
- Its arithmetic example is the [[hyperoperation]] {relation: example-of} hierarchy.
- It differs from [[analogy]] {relation: distinguished-from}: upward to a wider domain, against sideways to another one.
- It is what [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: example-of} does to Hofstadter's original sense.
- Its results, when proposed by a model, need [[evaluation]] {relation: uses}.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[induction]].** Inference from observations to a hypothesis. It is the
  operation most often confused with generalization, because its conclusions
  are usually general statements.
- **[[abstraction]].** Identifying shared structure apart from detail. It
  gives generalization the thing to extend.
- **[[definitions-of-strange-loop]].** Seven candidate definitions ordered by
  what each includes. They are successive generalizations and restrictions of
  one starting sense.
- **[[analogy]].** A partial structure-preserving mapping between domains.
  Where an analogy holds completely, the two domains become cases of one
  generalization.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[generalization]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[generalization]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[classify-operation]] would use this
card as the definition of the label `generalization`, and would have to keep
it apart from `induction`.

